the data on kovid has been looking so  good recently but is there now another  twist in the first two weeks of the year  the seven-day average of cases for the  uk more than halved but then leveled off  sharply with cases still far higher than  last january's peak new research gives  us some clue to what's going on  scientists behind the react one study  test around 100 000 people in england  every few weeks whether or not they've  got symptoms and their latest results  show that in adults young and old coed  rates soared before christmas driven by  omicron but have been falling since the  new year but that fall has now been  cancelled out by the rise in children  since they went back to school almost  eight percent one in every 12 had  coveted at the time the scientists were  testing in adults rates were lower  between two and a half and five percent  for different age groups so why are  children and adults out of sync well the  researchers think in december children  were still being infected with delta  omicron was racing through adults  because the vaccine was less protective  against the new variant now omigron has  pushed out delta and children are  vulnerable while the virus is coming up  against a wall of immunity in adults  [Music]  it's the second time nine-year-old sully  has come down with covid he had it last  summer now he's off school again it's  not unusual two-thirds of people testing  positive in the new study said they had  been infected before  i think at the rate his school friends  were coming down with it i wasn't overly  surprised when he tested positive for a  second time  and when you look at things logically  sully's age group are the only ones who  aren't vaccinated and aren't wearing  masks the hospital data so far is  looking better with a dip in admissions  but they could rise again if the virus  spreads from children to their parents  and then on to other adults but rates  are still a long way off from the levels  you get from flu the winter of 2018 was  the last bad year the hospitalizations  in england for flu peaked at 758 in a  week in just the last seven days there'd  been 11 000 admissions for covid it's  not yet like flu at all
 

